; Armoured trooper, ~1.55m to the top of the helmet, built from primitives.
; Origin at the feet. Everything above the waist hangs off Spine, which the
; player pitches so the weapon tracks where you are aiming. WeaponSocket is the
; attachment point -- swapping weapons means reparenting a different scene onto
; it.
;
; The read is plate over undersuit. Every load-bearing shape is a hard panel
; with a dark joint showing between it and the next one -- pauldrons over the
; shoulders, a sternum plate over the chest, segmented bands over the belly,
; thigh and shin plates strapped over the legs -- and the gaps between them are
; the near-black bodysuit underneath. That contrast is what makes armour look
; like armour rather than like a man painted grey: without the dark line between
; two plates they merge into one lump.
;
; The helmet has no face. A visor band across the eyes, a filter at the mouth
; and nothing else, which is worth more than a face would be: it reads instantly
; at fifty metres, it costs four meshes, and it means nobody has to look at what
; a face built out of boxes looks like.
;
; Every anchor the rest of the game reaches for is where it always was: hips at
; y=0.73, knees a 0.315 segment below, Spine at 0.84, the arm rig's shoulders at
; +-0.21 from it, and the bottom of the helmet on the 1.32 headshot line.
[gd_scene load_steps=44 format=3]

